 "finished_by_agent": true,
 "finish_summary": "Starting from the reference (a 32-term linear adder chain: area 264.7 \u00b5m\u00b2, WNS 7.016 ns, score 69.899), I established that the score is completely timing-dominated (10\u00b7WNS vs 0.001\u00b7area) and that a zero-logic probe caps WNS at ~7.86 ns, i.e. only ~0.8 ns of combinational popcount delay is available to optimize. Calibration probes (parity tree, small popcounts) gave ~0.03\u20130.05 ns per logic level. I then benchmarked many structurally different implementations with the cheap estimator: balanced +-tree (7.091), explicit gate-level ripple tree (7.118), 4\u00d7popcount8 + column CSA (7.123), 8\u00d7popcount4 + column reduction (7.200), a depth-scheduled Dadda with lookahead CPA (7.158), and Wallace-style carry-save trees (7.226\u20137.242). A/B tests showed ABC fully renormalizes local gate forms (identical results for (x&y)|((x|y)&z) vs (x&y)|((x^y)&z)), so only the global decomposition matters. The winner (V5) is a hand-scheduled carry-save tree using exactly 26 full adders (the theoretical minimum for 32\u21926) plus 5 half adders, with late-arriving bits deliberately placed on the \"z\" input of each adder and the two leftover bits folded into a level-2 FA, ending in a short 1-level-per-column carry ripple. It was formally proven equivalent and simulated clean, and post-route it gives area 246.8 \u00b5m\u00b2, WNS 7.182 ns, score 71.574 (+1.675 over baseline) with fewer cells (222) than the original. Three P&R submissions were used, each an improvement (71.417 \u2192 71.452 \u2192 71.574); no remaining candidate beat V5's pre-route estimate, so I stopped.",
